 to 6\ninches. Winds gusting as high as 50 mph.\n\n* WHERE...Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Feet.\n\n* WHEN...Until 11 AM MST this morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Travel could be very difficult, especially tonight and\nMonday morning over Wolf Creek Pass and Cumbres Pass. Strong winds\ncould produce areas of blowing and drifting snow.","ends":"2026-11-02T11:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.73e00f04c0d38244888300879b18131d33016053.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.73e00f04c0d38244888300879b18131d33016053.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Warning","headline":"Flood Warning issued November 1 at 4:37PM MST until November 2 at 4:30PM MST by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding along the South Fork Rio Grande and mainstem Rio\nGrande through South Fork caused by rain and snowmelt is expected.\n\n* WHERE...Southeastern Mineral County in south central Colorado...\nWest Central Rio Grande County in south central Colorado...\n\n* WHEN...Until 430 PM MST Monday.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding along the South Fork Rio Grande and mainstem Rio\nGrande through South Fork is expected. Additional flooding of\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations is\nimminent or occurring. Numerous roads remain closed due to\nflooding. Streams continue to rise due to excess runoff from\nrainfall and upstream snowmelt.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 433 PM MST, Rain and snowmelt is occurring, and will cause\nflooding. Between 1 and 2 inches of rain have fallen on top of\nmelting snow. Stream gauge data indicates that the South Fork Rio\nGrande at South Fork will be reaching flood stage soon.\n- Additional rainfall amounts of 1 to 3 inches are possible in the\nwarned area.\n- Some locations that will experience flooding include...\nSouth Fork and areas along Highway 160 up to Wolf Creek Pass.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T16:30:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Mineral, CO; Rio Grande, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.a1fe13ce206a35627c94a6be4c4adad3c2e93d4f.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.a1fe13ce206a35627c94a6be4c4adad3c2e93d4f.001.1"},{"event":"Avalanche Advisory","headline":"Avalanche Advisory issued October 31 at 7:16PM MDT by NWS Denver CO","description":"SABCO\n\nTHE FOLLOWING MESSAGE IS TRANSMITTED AT THE REQUEST OF THE\nCOLORADO AVALANCHE INFORMATION CENTER.\n\n...A SPECIAL AVALANCHE ADVISORY IN EFFECT FROM 4:30 PM SATURDAY\nUNTIL 4:30 PM MONDAY...\n\n*WHAT...Avalanche conditions are dangerous above treeline.\n*WHERE...West Elk Mountains, Elk Mountains, San Juan Mountains, and\nLa Plata Mountains\n*WHEN...From 4:30 PM Saturday until 4:30 PM Monday.\n*IMPACTS...Backcountry travelers can easily trigger dangerous\navalanches large enough to bury a human.\n\n*PRECAUTION/PREPAREDNESS ACTIONS...A potent winter storm increases\navalanche danger Sunday into Monday. Rain, snow, and wind will cause\nnatural avalanches above treeline. Check the forecast at\nwww.colorado.gov/avalanche.\n\nBeyer & Pritchett","ends":"2026-11-02T16:30:00-07:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"West Elk and Sawatch Mountains; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5d9a0ec4f46df7deb3c2c0e5015df58147db243f.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5d9a0ec4f46df7deb3c2c0e5015df58147db243f.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"PUB","issued":"2026-11-02T11:25:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KPUB 021125 AFDPUB Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Pueblo CO 425 AM MST Mon Nov 2 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Flooding continues across rivers along the eastern San Juans and adjacent San Luis Valley. - Gusty winds expected today, especially across the plains, with gusts of 30-50mph expected. - Unsettled pattern continues through the week and into the weekend. && .SHORT TE",